ORM (object relational mapping)
mapping between object and relational database

ORM frameworks
- Hibernate
- EclipseLink
- OpenJPA

===============================================================================
JPA (RuleBook) vs Hibernate (Player) vs Spring Data JPA (Coach, helps to use JPA/Hibernate more easily, provides shortcuts, saves effort)

JPA (Java Persistence API)
Its just a specification
provides set of guidelines on how Java programs should interact with databases
it itself doesn’t do the work — it only defines how things should be done

Example: It says “You should use @Entity to mark a class as a database table"

Hibernate
framework that follows JPA rules but also adds extra features beyond JPA
a real implementation that actually does the work of saving, updating, and fetching data from the database

Example: Hibernate knows how to take your Student class and turn it into SQL queries that talk to the database

Spring Data JPA
framework built on top of JPA
makes using JPA easier by reducing boilerplate code

Example:
no need to provide findByName implementation
Spring Data JPA generates the implementation class for us at runtime

public interface StudentRepository extends JpaRepository<Student, Integer> {
    List<Student> findByName(String name);
}

